cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "Donghyunchop (동현촙): a single-player pencil-sketch rescue-helicopter laboratory in the manner of Choplifter II, with the number of helicopters, the starting stage, the paper colour and the seating capacity set through the esoteric language Donghyun-lang"
type: software
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24486348"
version: "0.01"
date-released: "2026-11-24"
authors:
  - family-names: Kim
    given-names: Dong Hyun
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9883-0229"
    affiliation: "Inha University College of Medicine"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/Raspberry-Kim/donghyun-lab22"
url: "https://raspberry-kim.github.io/donghyun-lab22/"
abstract: >-
  Donghyunchop is a browser-based, noncommercial toy that follows the
  controls and rules of Choplifter II (Beam Software, Game Boy, 1991): a
  side-view rescue helicopter flown with the directional keys, with
  momentum and no gravity, a nose that turns left, front and right on a
  short tap, backward flight, a machine gun, a secondary weapon (bomb,
  missile or shield) and a damage bar. The player lands beside barracks
  and on rooftops, takes people aboard one at a time and carries them back
  to the base; each of the seven stages has a rescue target. The field is
  a wide 400x225 view rather than the original 160x144 screen, the seven
  stages are newly built, and hitting terrain causes damage instead of an
  immediate crash. The number of helicopters (1-9), the starting stage
  (1-7), the paper colour (rough cream or sketchbook white) and the
  seating capacity (4-16) are chosen through a program written in the
  esoteric programming language Donghyun-lang. Input is keyboard, an
  on-screen directional pad with buttons, and gamepad, with fullscreen.
  The one image file is the title on the briefing screen, written by hand
  by the author; every helicopter, person, vehicle, terrain and the paper
  grain is drawn by code as wobbly graphite strokes when the page opens,
  in greyscale on paper, using the pencil engine of Donghyuns. Clearing
  all seven stages is celebrated with falling petals and a short fanfare
  synthesised in the browser from the trio melody of Elgar's Pomp and
  Circumstance March No. 1 (1901, public domain); no recording is used.
  No graphics, sound, text, stage data or code of the original game is
  used or extracted; all numbers are the author's own. The MIT license
  applies to the Donghyunchop code and documentation; the page fonts are
  under the SIL Open Font License, and the handwritten title remains the
  work of its writer. See LICENSE and CREDITS.md.
keywords:
  - rescue helicopter game
  - side-scrolling
  - procedural drawing
  - esoteric programming language
  - JavaScript
